                <pb facs="0945" n="831" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg831"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.50 Am 3/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Houghton</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-08-29T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">August 29,
                                1922—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>115. Our 102 of July 24, 4 p.m. Department now believes that although we should
                    be ready to make an impartial economic investigation at any time when it may
                    appear feasible, the better way would be not to press the matter at present but
                    to let the proposal come from the Moscow authorities. Then we can make our
                    conditions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
